.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt SYSLOG 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm syslog ,
.Nm klogctl
.Nd read and control the kernel's log
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/klog.h
.Ft int
.Fn klogctl "int type" "char *buf" "int len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn syslog ,
number 103, which glibc and musl call
.Fn klogctl ,
reads the kernel's log ring, the records
.Pa /dev/bootlog
gives, each a line that begins with its priority as
.Ql < Ns Ar N Ns > ,
as BusyBox's
.Ic klogd
and util-linux's
.Ic dmesg
read it.
.Fa type
is the action:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0, 1
Close and open: nothing.
.It 2
Read: wait until the ring has records past the reader's place, which
every reader shares, then take at most
.Fa len
bytes of them into
.Fa buf
and move the place past them.
A place the ring has dropped begins at its oldest record.
.It 3
Read all: the last records that fit in
.Fa len
bytes since the last clear, whole records only.
.It 4
Read all, then clear.
.It 5
Clear: a later read all begins after what the ring now holds.
.It 6
Turn the screen down to the critical records.
.It 7
Turn the screen back to the level action 6 put aside.
.It 8
Show on the screen the records more urgent than
.Fa len ,
from 1 to 8.
.It 9
The bytes the reader has not taken.
.It 10
The size of the ring, 16384 bytes.
.El
.Pp
Every action but 0 and 1 needs a privileged caller, as Linux with
.Va kernel.dmesg_restrict
set has it, since the ring is
.Pa /dev/bootlog Ns 's ,
which is root's.
The ring does not wake a waiting reader, since records come from any
context in the kernel; a reader waiting for records looks again every
tenth of a second.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Actions 2, 3 and 4 return the bytes read, 9 and 10 the sizes, and the
others 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EINVAL
An action outside 0 to 10, a negative length, or a level outside 1 to 8.
.It Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa buf
is not the caller's to write.
.It Er EINTR
A signal ended the wait of action 2.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr busybox-sysklogd 8
